#: src/development/debuggers.md:3
msgid "Using a debugger"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:5
msgid ""
"This page is not about [configuring Zed's debugger](../debugger.md). It "
"covers how to use a debugger while developing Zed itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:8
msgid "Using Zed's built-in debugger"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:10
msgid ""
"While the Zed project is open you can open the `New Process Modal` and "
"select the `Debug` tab. There you can see two debug configurations to debug "
"Zed with, one for GDB and one for LLDB. Select the configuration you want "
"and Zed will build and launch the binary."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:12
msgid "GDB is not supported on Apple Silicon Macs."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:14
msgid "Release build profile considerations"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:16
msgid ""
"By default, builds using the release profile (the profile used for nightly, "
"preview, and stable) include limited debug info."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:18
msgid ""
"This is done by setting the `profile.(release).debug` field in the root "
"`Cargo.toml` field to `\"limited\"`."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:20
msgid ""
"The official documentation for the `debug` field is [here](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/cargo/reference/profiles.html#debug). In short, `\"limited\"` "
"strips type-level and variable-level debug info."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:23
msgid ""
"In release builds, this reduces binary size. Type-level and variable-level "
"debug info is not required for useful stack traces."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:25
msgid ""
"However, this data matters when you are actively debugging. Without it, "
"debuggers cannot resolve local variables, inspect values, or format output "
"with pretty-printers."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:27
msgid ""
"To get the full debugger experience on a release build, compile a Zed binary "
"with full debug info."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:29
msgid ""
"The simplest way is to use `--config` to override the `debug` field in the "
"root `Cargo.toml` when running `cargo run` or `cargo build`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:32 src/development/debuggers.md:33
msgid "'profile.release.debug=\"full\"'"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:36
msgid ""
"If you do not want to pass `--config` on every `cargo` command, you can also "
"change the section in the [root `Cargo.toml`](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/zed/blob/main/Cargo.toml)"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:38
msgid "from"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:40
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"[profile.release]\n"
"debug = \"limited\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:45
msgid "to"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:47
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"[profile.release]\n"
"debug = \"full\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:52
msgid ""
"This makes all invocations of `cargo run --release` or `cargo build --"
"release` compile with full debug info."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:54
msgid "**Warning:** Do not commit these changes."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:56
msgid "Running Zed with a shell debugger GDB/LLDB"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:58
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:60
msgid ""
"When you install Rust through rustup (the recommended setup for Zed "
"development; see your platform guide [here](../development.md)), rustup also "
"installs helper scripts for debugging Rust binaries."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:62
msgid "These scripts are `rust-gdb` and `rust-lldb`."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:64
msgid ""
"You can read more about these scripts [here](https://michaelwoerister.github."
"io/2015/03/27/rust-xxdb.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:66
msgid ""
"They are wrapper scripts around `gdb` and `lldb` that inject commands and "
"flags for Rust-specific features such as pretty-printers and type info."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:68
msgid ""
"To use `rust-gdb` or `rust-lldb`, install `gdb` or `lldb` on your system."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:70
msgid ""
"The [linked article](https://michaelwoerister.github.io/2015/03/27/rust-xxdb."
"html) notes that the minimum supported versions are GDB 7.7 and LLDB 310. In "
"practice, newer versions are usually better."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:72
msgid ""
"**Note**: `rust-gdb` is not installed by default on Windows because `gdb` "
"support there is unstable. Use `rust-lldb` instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:74
msgid ""
"If you are new to these tools, see the `gdb` docs [here](https://www.gnu.org/"
"software/gdb/) and the `lldb` docs [here](https://lldb.llvm.org/)."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:76
msgid "Usage with Zed"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:78
msgid ""
"After enabling full debug info and building with `cargo build`, run `rust-"
"gdb` or `rust-lldb` against the compiled Zed binary:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:85
msgid ""
"You can also attach to a running Zed process (for example, one started with "
"`cargo run`):"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:92
msgid "`<pid>` is the process ID of the Zed instance you want to attach to."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:94
msgid ""
"To find the PID, use your system's process tools, such as Task Manager on "
"Windows or Activity Monitor on macOS."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:96
msgid ""
"You can also run `ps aux | grep zed` on macOS and Linux, or `Get-Process | "
"Select-Object Id, ProcessName` in PowerShell on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:98
msgid "Debugging Panics and Crashes"
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:100
msgid ""
"Debuggers are useful for finding the cause of panics and crashes, including "
"in Zed."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:102
msgid ""
"By default, when a process attached to `gdb` or `lldb` hits an exception "
"such as a panic, the debugger stops at that point so you can inspect program "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:104
msgid ""
"The initial stop point is often in Rust standard library panic or exception "
"handling code, so you usually need to walk up the stack to find the root "
"cause."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:106
msgid ""
"In `lldb`, use `backtrace` with `frame select`. `gdb` provides equivalent "
"commands."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:108
msgid ""
"After the program stops on the exception, you usually cannot continue normal "
"execution. You can still move between stack frames and inspect variables and "
"expressions, which is often enough to identify the crash cause."
msgstr ""

#: src/development/debuggers.md:110
msgid ""
"You can find additional information on debugging Zed crashes [here](./"
"debugging-crashes.md)."
msgstr ""
